msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:48
msgid "creating, rebuilding, or restyling any page or post"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:55
msgid "Work a verify-page report down to nothing: which findings to fix first, why block addresses move under you after a structural edit, and when the page is actually done."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:56
msgid "a page exists and verify-page or lint-page reported findings to fix"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:79
msgid "# Fixing a page from its verify report — agent playbook"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:81
msgid "saddle/verify-page returns findings, a letter grade and a coverage caveat. The grade is honest about its own limits: a structural finding caps it at C and an \"ignored\" finding at B, because both mean the page is not doing what the markup says it does. Work them in the order below — fixing warnings first wastes the effort when a structural fix moves everything."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:83
msgid "## The order, and why it is this order"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:85
msgid "1. STRUCTURAL findings first. These say the block tree itself is wrong — something nested where it cannot be, or a container with nothing in it. Every other finding is measured against a tree that is about to change, so fixing anything else first is work you will redo."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:86
msgid "2. IGNORED findings next. An \"ignored\" finding means you set something that never took effect: an attribute the block does not have, a preset slug that matches nothing on this site, a style group the block does not support. The page looks unstyled and the markup looks fine, which is why this one is easy to miss and worth its own pass. The fix is almost always a slug from saddle/context-bundle instead of an invented value."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:87
msgid "3. ERRORS, then WARNINGS. By this point the tree is right and the styling is landing, so these are real judgements about the design rather than noise."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:89
msgid "## Addresses move — re-read before you address again"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:91
msgid "Findings are addressed the same way saddle/get-blocks addresses nodes. Those addresses are positional, so ANY structural edit — adding, removing or moving a block — shifts every address after it. An address you read before that edit now points somewhere else."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:92
msgid "So: make one structural change, then call saddle/get-blocks again before touching the next one. Batching several structural edits against one read is the single most common way to damage a page while trying to fix it."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:93
msgid "Non-structural edits (saddle/edit-block on attributes or text) do not move anything, so those can be batched safely."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:95
msgid "## Working the loop"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:97
msgid "- Re-run saddle/verify-page after each pass. Stop when the score stops improving, not when it hits a number you like."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:98
msgid "- If a finding survives a fix you believe in, read the node with saddle/get-blocks and check what actually saved. A refused write leaves the page exactly as it was, so \"I fixed it\" and \"it is fixed\" are different claims."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:99
msgid "- saddle/lint-page is the design-quality half on its own, without the scoring. Use it when you want the findings but not the grade."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:101
msgid "## When it is done"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:103
msgid "No structural and no ignored findings, and you have opened saddle/get-preview-url and looked at the page. The report is server-side only and says so in its own coverage caveat — it cannot see overlap, contrast in context, or a section that is simply ugly. A page can score well and still be wrong, and that judgement is yours to make with your eyes."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:138
msgid "2. LOOK AT THE SITE — saddle/get-template on the header and footer parts. What wraps every page tells you the width, the spacing and the tone you are designing into. Skipping this is how a page ends up looking bolted on."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:139
msgid "2. LOOK AT THE SITE — this is a classic theme, so the header and footer live in theme files Saddle does not read. Open saddle/get-blocks on an existing page instead: what is already there tells you the width, the spacing and the tone you are designing into. Skipping this is how a page ends up looking bolted on."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:142
msgid "# Building a page in WordPress — agent playbook"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:144
msgid "A write call returning success is not evidence the page is right. The verify score is, and even that only covers what a server can check. Work the loop below; do not stop at the first successful write."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:146
msgid "## The workflow — in this order"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:148
msgid "1. ORIENT — saddle/context-bundle. One call gives you the design system, the blocks worth using, the theme's patterns, the site's templates and the section recipes. Call it once per session, not per page."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:150
msgid "3. PLAN — pick a starting point before composing anything. A theme pattern (saddle/insert-block-pattern) beats a section recipe, because it already carries this theme's styling. A recipe (saddle/get-section-recipe) beats hand-stacking blocks. Hand-stacking is the last resort, not the default."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:151
msgid "4. BUILD — saddle/set-blocks for a whole page, saddle/add-block and saddle/edit-block for surgical changes. Read saddle/get-block-schema before using a block type you have not used: it carries the placement rules and a worked example."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:152
msgid "5. STYLE WITH THE SITE'S OWN VALUES — use the slugs from the design system, never raw hex or pixel values. {\"backgroundColor\":\"<slug>\"}, {\"textColor\":\"<slug>\"}, {\"fontSize\":\"<slug>\"}. A page built from slugs follows the site when the owner changes their palette; a page built from hex values does not."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:153
msgid "6. VERIFY — saddle/verify-page. Fix in this order: structural findings, then \"ignored\" ones (styling that never took effect), then errors, then warnings. Addresses match saddle/get-blocks, and they SHIFT after a structural edit, so re-read before addressing again. Re-run until the score stops improving."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:154
msgid "7. LOOK — saddle/get-preview-url and open it. The score is explicitly not a visual sign-off: a page can pass every server-side check and still look wrong. This step is not optional."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:156
msgid "## Rules the server enforces (you cannot talk your way past these)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:158
msgid "- Invalid block markup is rejected, not saved. A refused write leaves the page exactly as it was."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:159
msgid "- Placement rules are real: a core/button must be inside core/buttons, a core/list-item inside core/list. get-block-schema tells you which."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:160
msgid "- Deleting or overwriting anything takes two calls: a preview, then the same call again with the confirm_token. Nothing is destroyed in one step."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:161
msgid "- Pages built with a page builder are refused by the native block tools. That is deliberate; editing them with the wrong toolset destroys the layout."
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:163
msgid "## Design quality bar — what \"designed\" means in numbers"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:196
msgid "## Avoid the generated look"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:198
msgid "- Three identical cards in a row, each with a button and nothing emphasised, is the tell. Feature one. A pricing section that recommends nothing makes the visitor do the work. (The lint rule \"no-featured-plan\" will say so.)"
msgstr ""

#: includes/class-saddle-playbook.php:199
msgid "- Every section full-width and centered, one after another, reads as a document rather than a page. Vary the rhythm: let something break the width, or pair text against media."
